{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Strictly protected reefs (e.g., Cabo Pulmo) exhibit a weaker negative relationship between annual mean SST and total fish productivity than unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether protection status modulates the sensitivity of reef productivity to SST variation.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, sum 'prod' to get annual total productivity.  \n2. Merge annual productivity with reef‐level mean_sst and protection_status (cabo pulmo, area protegida, sin proteccion).  \n3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: total_prod ~ mean_sst * protection_status + (1 | id_reef).  \n4. Examine the interaction term mean_sst:protection_status to test if protected reefs show a smaller slope.  \n5. Validate model assumptions (residual normality, homoscedasticity) and, if needed, apply transformations or robust estimation.","deliverables":"1. Table of model coefficients with interaction p‐values.  \n2. Marginal effect plots of total_prod vs mean_sst for each protection_status.  \n3. Diagnostic plots for mixed‐effects model."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs at higher latitudes exhibit a greater increase in mean fish body size over time compared to lower‐latitude reefs, reflecting differential size‐structure shifts under warming.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess how the trend in mean fish size over years varies along the latitudinal gradient.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, compute biomass‐weighted mean fish size: sum(size * biomass) / sum(biomass).  \n2. Retrieve the reef’s latitude (degree).  \n3. Fit a linear mixed model: mean_size ~ year * degree + (1 | id_reef).  \n4. Test the year:degree interaction—positive coefficient indicates stronger size increases at higher latitudes.  \n5. Plot predicted mean_size trajectories for low, mid, and high latitude reefs.","deliverables":"1. Mixed‐model summary with interaction term test.  \n2. Trajectory plot of mean_size vs year at representative latitudes.  \n3. Table of estimated slopes of size~year for quartile latitude reefs."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Deep transects (20 m) show lower interannual species turnover (Jaccard dissimilarity) than shallow transects (5 m).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare year‐to‐year community stability between depth strata.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐depth stratum, assemble presence/absence matrices of species by year.  \n2. For each reef‐depth, compute Jaccard dissimilarity between successive years.  \n3. Average annual dissimilarities per reef-depth.  \n4. Use a paired or unpaired t‐test (or Wilcoxon test) to compare mean turnover between shallow vs deep.  \n5. Optionally model turnover ~ depth + (1 | id_reef) in a mixed‐effects framework.","deliverables":"1. Table of mean Jaccard turnover per reef-depth.  \n2. Statistical test results comparing shallow vs deep.  \n3. Boxplot of turnover by depth stratum."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Annual reef‐level production‐to‐biomass ratio (prod/biomass) increases as total standing biomass declines, indicating compensatory productivity in depleted systems.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for an inverse relationship between standing biomass and P/B ratio across reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, compute total_prod and total_biomass by summing 'prod' and 'biomass'.  \n2. Compute PBR = total_prod / total_biomass.  \n3. Fit a nonlinear model or generalized additive model: PBR ~ s(total_biomass).  \n4. Examine the shape of the relationship—expect negative slope at high biomass.  \n5. Control for covariates (mean_sst, protection_status) in the model.","deliverables":"1. Fitted model summary and significance of smoothed term.  \n2. Plot of PBR vs biomass with fitted curve.  \n3. Table of predicted PBR at biomass quantiles."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher chlorophyll‐a concentrations support a larger biomass proportion of low‐trophic‐level (planktivorous) fishes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether pelagic subsidy (mean_chla) drives planktivore biomass dominance.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, sum biomass for species with trophic_level ≤2.5 (planktivores) and total biomass.  \n2. Compute prop_planktivore = biomass_low / total_biomass.  \n3. Merge with mean_chla, degree, protection_status.  \n4. Fit a beta‐regression or logistic‐transformed linear model: prop_planktivore ~ mean_chla + degree + protection_status.  \n5. Test positive effect of mean_chla on prop_planktivore.","deliverables":"1. Regression table with coefficient for mean_chla.  \n2. Partial effect plot of prop_planktivore vs mean_chla.  \n3. Diagnostic plots for model fit."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Strictly protected reefs exhibit more even species richness among trophic bins (low, mid, high) than unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare evenness of trophic‐bin species richness across protection statuses.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef (pooling all years), count unique species in each trophic_bin.  \n2. Compute Shannon evenness E = H/log(3) across the three bins.  \n3. Assign each reef’s protection_status.  \n4. Conduct ANOVA or Kruskal‐Wallis test: evenness ~ protection_status.  \n5. Perform post‐hoc pairwise comparisons (Tukey or Dunn).","deliverables":"1. Table of mean evenness by protection_status.  \n2. ANOVA table or nonparametric test results.  \n3. Boxplot of evenness across protection categories."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"After marine heatwave years (mean_sst > 95th percentile), species richness recovers faster in protected reefs than in unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess community resilience to extreme SST anomalies under different protection regimes.",  
      "steps":"1. Identify heatwave years per reef: years when reef’s mean_sst exceeds its 95th percentile across time.  \n2. For each reef and each event, record species richness for the two years before and three years after the heatwave.  \n3. Fit a segmented regression of richness ~ year_relative_event for each reef, extracting post‐event slope.  \n4. Compare post‐event slopes across protection_status using ANOVA or mixed model: slope ~ protection_status + (1|region).  \n5. Test if protected reefs have significantly higher recovery slopes.","deliverables":"1. Table of per‐reef recovery slopes and protection_status.  \n2. Statistical test comparing slopes.  \n3. Plot of mean richness trajectory relative to event for protected vs unprotected."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Average fish body size is significantly larger in early survey months (August–September) than in later months (October–December), reflecting seasonal recruitment pulses of small juveniles.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for seasonal shifts in community size structure within survey months.",  
      "steps":"1. Classify each survey into season: early (month 8–9) vs late (10–12).  \n2. For each reef‐season‐year, compute biomass‐weighted mean size.  \n3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: mean_size ~ season + depth + (1|id_reef) + (1|year).  \n4. Test the fixed effect of season (early vs late).  \n5. Plot seasonal boxplots of mean_size by depth.","deliverables":"1. Mixed‐model summary showing seasonal effect.  \n2. Boxplots of mean_size by season and depth.  \n3. Estimated seasonal difference in mean size with confidence interval."  
    }  
  }  
]}
